      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we start by noting that by definition, \\(\\sin A\\) is the ratio of the length of the side opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. However, in this case, we are asked to find \\(\\sin A\\) for \\(\\angle A\\) in the context of the right triangle, which simplifies our problem.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\)\n- \\(AB = 16\\) (one of the legs)\n- \\(BC = 24\\) (the hypotenuse)\n\nIn a right triangle, the sine of \\(\\angle A\\) (which is \\(90^\\circ\\)) is always 1 because \\(\\sin 90^\\circ = 1\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
