    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), \\(AB = 16\\), and \\(BC = 24\\), we need to recognize that in a right triangle with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), \\(\\sin A\\) is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to \\(\\angle A\\) to the length of the hypotenuse. Since \\(\\angle A\\) is the right angle, it is not one of the angles in question, but it affects the hypotenuse. However, \\(\\sin A\\) is still defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to \\(C\\) (not \\(A\\)) to the length of the hypotenuse \\(BC\\).\n\nIn this problem, let's assume that we need to find \\(\\sin B\\) because \\(\\angle B\\) is the angle opposite to the side of length 16. Here, the hypotenuse \\(BC\\) is 24 and the opposite side to \\(\\angle B\\) is 16. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin B = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin B\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\).",
