    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "In a right triangle, the sine of an angle is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the hypotenuse. In triangle $ABC$, with $\\angle A = 90^\\circ$, $AB$ is the opposite side to $\\angle C$, and $BC$ is the hypotenuse. We are given that $AB = 16$ and $BC = 24$.\n\nTo find $\\sin C$, we first find the length of the third side, $AC$. Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\nBC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n24^2 = 16^2 + AC^2\n\\]\n\\[\n576 = 256 + AC^2\n\\]\nSolving for $AC^2$:\n\\[\nAC^2 = 576 - 256\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 320\n\\]\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{320} = 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\nNow, we can find $\\sin C$:\n\\[\n\\sin C = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the value of $\\sin A$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
